// Test: stdlib/json/json_encode_typed_list
// Grammar: function_call → namespace "." function_name "(" args ")"
// Semantic: FUNC001 (tracking: FUNC-BUILTIN) — json.encode on a typed List<T> walks the native
//   list heap layout and emits a JSON array directly, dispatching per element
//   type tag for correct primitive formatting.
// Regression: discovered while fixing JSON-ENCODE-PAIRS-MALFORMED (compiler
//   0.30.298). Before the fix:
//     - `list<string> = ["a","b","c"]; json.encode(items)` produced
//       `[null,0.000…02,null]` because list elements are stored raw (not boxed
//       Any) but the generic stringify_array path expected boxed Any pointers.
//   Fix: `json.encode(List<T>)` routes to the new `__json_encode_cln_list`
//   stdlib helper, which knows the native Clean list layout and the per-tag
//   element decoding (Integer / Boolean / Number / String).
//
// Earlier versions of this test were split one-list-per-file to work around
// LIST-ALLOCATE-HEAP-POINTER-COLLISION (compiler 0.30.302) — the list bump
// allocator used `mem[0]` as its heap pointer and let every list overlap at
// address 0. Once that was fixed (along with the list.push return-value drop
// in the codegen and the f64-stride under-allocation), multiple list literals
// can safely coexist, so the per-element-tag cases live in this one file.
//
// Expected output:
//   ["a","b","c"]
//   [1,2,3]
//   [true,false,true]
//   [1.5,2.5,3.5]
//   []

start:
	list<string> ss = ["a", "b", "c"]
	list<integer> ns = [1, 2, 3]
	list<boolean> bs = [true, false, true]
	list<number> fs = [1.5, 2.5, 3.5]
	list<string> empty = []
	print(json.encode(ss))
	print(json.encode(ns))
	print(json.encode(bs))
	print(json.encode(fs))
	print(json.encode(empty))
